{"title":"2001 IMO Shortlist Problems/N4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cd1930638e05b2b7561088ad7bebf4c648d8298.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\geq 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> be a prime number. Prove that there exists an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/8/c983c79c649f70d2b14b0e86e525fb3a59862296.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\leq a \\leq p - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"15\" /> such that neither <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/4/37482ca4f540d05f1384186fcd8eb962c7032259.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{p - 1} - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"64\" height=\"15\" /> nor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/b/aab4308f3b944f77a046ce811b8d6a42785b3c56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a + 1)^{p - 1} - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"21\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591f689f950feb4a9cff58ff3166d915f916a405.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_.28Elementary_Group_Theory.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_(Elementary_Group_Theory)\">Solution (Elementary Group Theory)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution (Elementary Group Theory)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let us work in the group <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/6552a1e04eed40f19f28df2d85a3adfa00ec362a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\mathbb{Z}/p^2\\mathbb{Z})^{\\times}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>Note that the order of this group is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/9/469006548c055509c7752e0dd210b544fcb45367.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi({p^2})=p^2-p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"19\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/6552a1e04eed40f19f28df2d85a3adfa00ec362a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\mathbb{Z}/p^2\\mathbb{Z})^{\\times}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"19\" /> is cyclic, we know that it is isomorphic to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/6/966ae93fad05f7d9dcc942882288a6824895b1f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\mathbb{Z}/(p^2-p)\\mathbb{Z})^{+}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"19\" />. \n</p><p>We can then conclude how many residues <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> there are such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/2/6f24d56f373ea4971b945d77585c72ec5d77d6fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p-1)n\\equiv0\\text{ (mod }p^2-p\\text{)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"201\" height=\"19\" />. For some arbitrary natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, one has:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58c96b21ef29d90a78a9d4d98eed68a3c5893632.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p-1)n=k(p^2-p)\\]\" width=\"158\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/e/38e7bdb84e2562c315c1cdb73705b318a769b128.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n=kp\\]\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" />\nTherefore there are only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> possible values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. It's also notable that if this is true for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/f/b2f0c6d216bb63a89845904c513ec0007b6b3e7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />, then it is also true for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/1/531f8c9772ca98eeeb178390d8daf8652d29bd47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2-p-n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/8/518ebbccd4993b5dc6045bbfd288390d58d418d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" />. This implies that there are also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> different values of a such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/0/7a042c4111be2f328c5d7b23980a4bbcb1e37daf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{p-1}\\equiv 1\\text{ (mod }p^2\\text{)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"138\" height=\"19\" />. It also implies that if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/5/ee5c7c921b3334f6ecf58c241eeea42acf0ce71e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> has this property, so does <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/4/ac49150515e4494a0829e905f425586cc5c53c3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{a_0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"23\" height=\"41\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/1/fa1c642486402ea182e31cb5821d3c50b16da41f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"15\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/0/aa072512ce5af659e11073a22fc0a2aa2aa3dd11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{a_0^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"23\" height=\"42\" /> when working in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/6552a1e04eed40f19f28df2d85a3adfa00ec362a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\mathbb{Z}/p^2\\mathbb{Z})^{\\times}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"19\" />. The squares also have their inverses which are guaranteed to have the property.\n</p><p>There exists no numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/7/237d72416192d5e78a94fdce7852ddc939de09f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;m_1,m_2\\leq p-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"152\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/7/c47716282dda83ef325acd1007a0deb278c0d6ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m_1m_2\\equiv1\\text{ (mod }p^2\\text{)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"151\" height=\"19\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/c/f3caef29506f63e0c0e3ddba8a3a006e47697e06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p-2)(p-2)&lt;p^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"148\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/7/da7ae9ddae278775a86aa7194241268ec2060bad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />. Therefore, at most half of the values where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/7/cd7d8969f1abaade2c0cbf18a93a676a38c02898.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0^{p-1}\\equiv 1\\text{ (mod }p^2\\text{)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> are in range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/4/28465132f22a6c0d3147600517026ab116e2f1f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;a_0\\leq p-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" />. We can again remove some of the potential values by squaring all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/c/89c978e8da9dc85783afd243ce766589a92d74ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0\\geq\\sqrt{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> and taking their inverse. As long as no more than half of the values in that range can have the required property, there must be a pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/b/64be5731bafe97ab8fb5d0f9c52c1c5362f963d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> that satisfy the requirements by the pigeonhole principle.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/e/abe3cbc49c1da929dc790c7f0f1894b62a6b5701.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{p-\\sqrt(p-2)-1}{2}\\leq\\frac{p-3}{2}\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c738a0cc84c2f8fc3737ce8c8d34311601da3c7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\leq(\\sqrt(p-2))\\]\" width=\"117\" height=\"32\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/f/88f8aeb8836b37b217f68a742e50feca42904a65.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p\\geq6\\]\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Lastly, you must show that there is a pair for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/4/9744b986a275413b1d5e52af4ee4642253f606d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />. This is satisfied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55afdab16c8c49089c4d9ceeb8d3c8cf1f088be6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d9eedc16960b6dd93b8f6225e43d0140bc26038.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^{4}\\equiv16\\text{ (mod }25\\text{)}\\]\" width=\"131\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/2/1320b068a1515627e45bc8933ef126d355bca950.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3^{4}\\equiv6\\text{ (mod }25\\text{)}\\]\" width=\"121\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2001 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2001 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?t=17473\">Discussion on AOPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018045158\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.020 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.036 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 223/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 7858/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7200:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018045158 and revision id 249974. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}